== 2026-11-20 12:33:58 #1025 route (c): why the hionly control of the D 17.5 dd sign test did not fire. Written before the run.
Facts (lane_dd17.log, dd_hybrid.log): at k_c 55, D 17.5 the dd M reads -1 at all 6 lambda (1.935-1.965), variants agree, robust to
noise 1e-24..1e-16. The control --mutate=hionly (the dd build and LU in double arithmetic) also reads -1 at 1.9505, 1.9515, 1.955, with
ln|det| 0.12-0.14 above the dd one, while the float64 build (DSSEll, complex step) reads +, -, + there with ln|det| off by 1.3-2.5.
So by its criteria the sign test's reading is void. The hybrid (float64 blocks 0-11, dd block 12) matches the dd M's sign at all 6 lambda
and its ln|det| to <= 2.5e-5 (H1 passes, its float64 control fires).
Hypothesis: the float64 build's sign errors come from its block 12 (the horizon series to the last node), not from double rounding as
such; the ddm algorithm's block 12 in double arithmetic (hionly) is much closer to the dd one.
Run (dd_b12_cause.py, venv-1025dd): at lambda 1.9505 and 1.955, block 12 three ways: float64 DSSEll, the dd build, and the dd build under
the hionly patch (applied in-process after the dd build, the same wrapper as ell_ddm's --mutate=hionly, then a fresh DDEll).
Report: max|B - B_dd| / max|B_dd| and the largest column-wise ||b - b_dd|| / ||b_dd|| for B = float64 and B = hionly; the dets by
multidouble dd_logdet of (a) float64 blocks 0-11 + dd block 12, (b) float64 blocks 0-11 + hionly block 12, (c) the plain float64 M.
Consistency check that can fail: (a) reproduces dd_hybrid.log's ln|det| at these lambda to 1e-8.
Prediction: the float64 block 12's error exceeds the hionly one's by >= 100x, and (b) reads -1 at both lambda.
Reading: if both hold, the control failed to fire because the float64 build's error is in its horizon block's construction, and the
float64 rounding of the ddm algorithm does not flip the sign in 1.935-1.965; a void reading of the dd sign test stays void, and the next
dd check needs a control that is shown to flip a sign (proposal goes to t5). If (b) reads +, the hypothesis is wrong.
